1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,400
Renters in America paid 772 billion dollars in rent last year.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:08,000
Nearly one in four fell behind at least once.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:12,000
That's not bad tenants: half of renter households spend over

4
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,800
thirty percent of their income on housing.

5
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:19,600
Today a property manager's toolkit is a late fee,

6
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:22,000
a notice on the door, and

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,400
3.6 million eviction filings a year.

8
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:28,400
It's slow, it costs everyone, and it loses the tenant.

9
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:32,400
Only one in five operators is very satisfied with it.

10
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
What's missing is a conversation the day rent goes late.

11
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,800
Nobody has time to make that call.

12
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:43,000
So we built Mia, an AI voice agent.

13
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,700
[live call] Mia: Hi, is this John? It's Mia from Sunset Properties.

14
00:00:45,700 --> 00:00:48,367
[live call] John: Yeah.

15
00:00:48,367 --> 00:00:51,067
[live call] Mia: Your tap repair is booked for Thursday.

16
00:00:51,067 --> 00:00:53,767
[live call] Mia: The main reason I'm calling is your rent.

17
00:00:53,767 --> 00:00:56,433
[live call] Mia: October's $2,400 is unpaid, and August and September both came in late.

18
00:00:56,433 --> 00:00:59,133
[live call] John: Thursday? I've been waiting two weeks on that leaky faucet.

19
00:00:59,133 --> 00:01:01,800
[live call] John: And honestly, I can't cover the rent. My hours got cut.

20
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:04,500
[live call] Mia: I've texted you the city rental assistance link and helpline.

21
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:07,200
[live call] SMS: sf.gov/renthelp

22
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:09,867
[live call] John: Half. $1,200.

23
00:01:09,867 --> 00:01:12,567
[live call] Mia: $1,200 today and the rest by the 18th.

24
00:01:12,567 --> 00:01:15,267
[live call] John: Yes. Send it.

25
00:01:15,267 --> 00:01:17,933
[live call] Mia: It's on your phone.

26
00:01:17,933 --> 00:01:20,633
[live call] Mia: $1,200 received.

27
00:01:20,633 --> 00:01:23,300
[live call] John: Nobody's ever actually called me before.

28
00:01:23,300 --> 00:01:26,000
[live call] Mia: Thanks, John.

29
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
While Mia talks, the manager's dashboard updates live: rent recovered,

30
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,800
still overdue, promised on plans, and how

31
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:35,200
fast the first call went out.

32
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:38,800
A short list shows what actually needs a person.

33
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:42,800
Every call is there, step by step, with the transcript.

34
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,800
John gets a plan that fits his

35
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:48,200
payday instead of a late-fee letter.

36
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:52,600
The manager gets paid today, with no notice and no filing.

37
00:01:52,600 --> 00:01:56,200
64 billion dollars of rent comes due every month.

38
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:59,400
Some of it just needs a phone call.

39
00:01:59,400 --> 00:01:59,800
RentRecovery.
